Okay, hi everyone, let's get underway. Welcome to our All Hands meeting. We have these every Friday.

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
This one looks a bit different than normal. It's like a glimpse into our future when there are a lot more of us.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
And we're all much older, obviously.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Anyway, we thought we'd model today on a school parents evening, so in a minute we'll be giving out the report cards for your children.

5
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:39,000
You get five minutes with our team leader while they try to remember which one your child is, and then a cup of coffee.

6
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Just kidding. What we'd actually like to do is tell you and show you what it is we actually do at Paul's Swigger.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:55,000
So if you're anything like my parents, you have a fairly superficial understanding of what it is that your children actually do at work.

8
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Something to do with computers is about as deep as it normally goes.

9
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:08,000
So what we'd like to do today is show you what it actually is that your amazing children do at this amazing company.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:23,000
I'm not going to do all the talking. I've asked quite a lot of our swiggers to speak up and everyone's going to tell us very briefly about one thing that we do.

11
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:31,000
We'll start with Burp Suite Pro. So this is where our story began. I first wrote it back in 2003.

12
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
It's come a long way. Today it is the leading software used by web security testers everywhere.

13
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:43,000
And more importantly, it has a team now of proper software developers who are looking after it.

14
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:48,000
And Raji is going to tell us some more.

15
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
This year, we're focusing on improving the user experience of our product.

16
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
A good example of this is how we're helping users get started.

17
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Did I go too fast? As you can see, that's the start page for one of our tools called the Intruder.

18
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:08,000
It's quite confusing. We don't know where to start from or what to do next.

19
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Now, we've introduced a new start page which has tips and links to documentation.

20
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,000
And also the new start page is a lot easier to understand and use.

21
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Thanks, Raji. Now, Joni is going to give us an example of another nice Burp Suite Pro.

22
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Improvement we've made recently.

23
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Yes, hi guys. I always want to say this in all hands. Hi, Mom.

24
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:45,000
And so a problem we have with Burp is that, or one of many problems we have, is that many users complain that it's slow and that it freezes.

25
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:50,000
But sometimes it's not necessarily our fault.

26
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,000
We allow users to install extensions and often they install too many.

27
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:56,000
A bit like having too many apps open on your phone.

28
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
So we introduced a feature to allow users to see if their extensions are slowing Burp down.

29
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Hopefully they'll install a few and we'll get fewer complaints.

30
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Thanks, Joni. So that was Burp Suite Pro.

31
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Our other main software product we make is called Burp Suite Enterprise Edition.

32
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,000
This was born four years ago in 2018.

33
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:26,000
It's used by companies everywhere who need to scan their websites for vulnerabilities or who are creating secure websites.

34
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
And Mary is going to show us where Enterprise Edition is up to today.

35
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Hi, everybody.

36
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:45,000
So Enterprise provides integrations with external systems, including several issue tracking applications.

37
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,000
These are typically used by software teams to manage their website development.

38
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:57,000
So a customer can connect Enterprise with these issue tracking systems, so that when a scan of their website finds security issues,

39
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:00,000
it automatically reports these in the external system.

40
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
This means software teams will have the information to fix the problem in their own systems as soon as it is detected.

41
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Thanks, Mary. We're constantly improving all our products and Chris is going to give us an example of how we've been improving Enterprise Edition.

42
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Hello. So this was the page that was shown when a scan completes.

43
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:37,000
It shows a breakdown of the vulnerabilities that were found for that particular site, along with other data such as the URLs that were scanned.

44
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:46,000
The problem was that information was lacking in certain areas and certain information was hard to digest, such as the bar charts that are using stacked values.

45
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:51,000
So we redesigned the page to make data a lot more visual and a lot more accessible.

46
00:04:51,000 --> 00:05:00,000
And we also introduced a severity trend so that users can see the results of the current scan versus previous runs so that they can be sure that they're making their websites more secure.

47
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Thanks, Chris.

48
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,000
Now inside both the editions of Burp Suite is Burp Scanner.

49
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:18,000
This does the incredibly difficult job of automatically finding security vulnerabilities in websites.

50
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:21,000
And this is one of the hardest problems we work on.

51
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Fortunately, we have a team of incredibly clever people who are working on it.

52
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
And Tom is going to tell us what they've been up to recently.

53
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:40,000
So unfortunately, the better websites look, the harder they are to crawl.

54
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
So back in the day, a page would have links and those links would take you to new pages.

55
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,000
Pausing these links and figuring out where they go is easy.

56
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:53,000
But nowadays, clicking anything on a page could cause anything to change.

57
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:00,000
So we now use a browser to crawl websites exactly as a user would, asking it what's clickable and what's changed rather than trying to figure it out ourselves.

58
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:06,000
So this theoretically means any website usable by a human is crawlable by us.

59
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Thanks, Tom.

60
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:17,000
When you're making software to solve exceptionally hard problems, it's very easy for things to go wrong.

61
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Alex is going to explain how we approach that challenge.

62
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:27,000
So yeah, this is the worrying view quite a few of our customers had.

63
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
A lot of red and not much information.

64
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
It's like with most things at Portsville, we turn to data.

65
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
So the bad news was about one in five scans were failing.

66
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:39,000
But the somewhat good news was that three quarters of that were down to user error.

67
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:43,000
So they're quite easy problems to solve as opposed to the deep, hard technical problems.

68
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:46,000
So we had a lot of sessions to try and think of good solutions.

69
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:52,000
And this is one of them that we've implemented about including in-page remediation advice for any failures.

70
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Thanks, Alex.

71
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:02,000
Lots of software companies make great software.

72
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:07,000
Not many of them constantly push the boundaries of what is even possible in their space.

73
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:10,000
And James is going to explain how we do exactly that.

74
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:24,000
Sometimes malicious hacker group discovers an entire new attack technique.

75
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:29,000
And this is really scary because nobody else even knows this technique exists.

76
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:33,000
So even experts don't know how to defend against it.

77
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:39,000
So on the research team, we try and find these techniques first before the bad guys do.

78
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:48,000
And then we share the full details through presentations and white papers so that everyone understands these attacks and knows how to defend against them.

79
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:57,000
After we warned companies about this particular technique, some of them voluntarily gave us over $100,000 to say thanks.

80
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:03,000
And Portswigger then matched this and donated $200,000 to the International Red Cross to support Ukraine.

81
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:11,000
Thanks, James.

82
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:17,000
You can now tell your friends that you've met the best web security vulnerability researcher in the world.

83
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:22,000
Now, it's actually fairly easy to make a rubbish vulnerability scanner.

84
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:28,000
And it's fairly hard for a non-specialist to know whether any given scanner is any good.

85
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:36,000
Some of our competitors like to pretend that their products are as good as ours by saying essentially,

86
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,000
yeah, you've got a vulnerability scanner, so have we.

87
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:44,000
Which is a bit like saying two cars are equivalent because they both have an engine.

88
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:50,000
So we need a way of proving to people that our engine is better than anyone else's.

89
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
And Matt's going to tell us how we go about doing that.

90
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:01,000
Thanks, Daph.

91
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
So we have a problem.

92
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:07,000
At the core of both of our products is the world's best vulnerability scanner.

93
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
But this is a really abstract concept to show.

94
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
So what we needed was a benchmark.

95
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:14,000
So our Academy team built one.

96
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:17,000
Now, this is called a deliberately vulnerable web app.

97
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:21,000
And what we want people to do is to scan it just to see how good our scanner is.

98
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:25,000
So what we're doing is showing it off all over social media to get them to do that.

99
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:26,000
Thanks.

100
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:32,000
Thanks, Matt.

101
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,000
Now, it's a crowded marketplace.

102
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:38,000
And when we build amazing things, we need to work hard to be heard.

103
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:40,000
And Emma is going to tell us how we approach that.

104
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:52,000
So maximising the value that our audience gets from Portswiga is always the aim.

105
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:56,000
But as you've probably seen, we're often working with some pretty heavily technical stuff.

106
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:09,000
So we have to really carefully craft a message that strikes a really core balance between this really heavy technical message and allowing our audience to understand what the heck it is we've even done or what we're talking about.

107
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:13,000
And we need to keep our technical street cred because we're pretty proud of that.

108
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:18,000
Two years ago on our LinkedIn platform, we had 2000 followers thereabouts.

109
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,000
We've now got 62,000.

110
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,000
So I think we're going some way towards generating a clear message.

111
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
If you want to find out more, follow us on LinkedIn.

112
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:26,000
Thank you.

113
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
Thanks, Emma.

114
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:39,000
Now, as customers dig a bit deeper into learning about our products and using them, we often need to explain how to use them.

