We call this the SWIG factor.

214
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:13,000
We turn away lots of people who would be highly capable in their area of expertise because they fall short in these areas.

215
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:19,000
And we do believe the way our people behave and work together is a superpower.

216
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:22,000
And our culture is something that we obsess about.

217
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
And Becky is going to tell us about one way we do that.

218
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:36,000
So I want to tell you about the amazing job that our team leaders do.

219
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:40,000
At Portswiger, we think about leadership in a slightly different way.

220
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:41,000
So our leaders don't tell.

221
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:42,000
They don't direct.

222
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:45,000
They're here as a supporting function in many ways.

223
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
They're here to enable their team success.

224
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
They solve problems.

225
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:53,000
They allow everyone in their team to do to fulfill their potential.

226
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:59,000
And when the team does their amazing things that they do, they deflect the praise onto their team where it's deserved.

227
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
And this is a really difficult role.

228
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:06,000
And it's one that they fully embrace and they continually try and raise the bar in everything they do.

229
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:11,000
And once a year, we take the opportunity to gather feedback for them and see how they're doing.

230
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:14,000
And the results you're seeing here were the results from our survey last year.

231
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:20,000
And with an average score of 8.8 across every attribute in which we ask people for their feedback,

232
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:25,000
which shows you the depth and the consistency of their leadership skills.

233
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:28,000
And these results are off the scale.

234
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:34,000
There are so few leadership teams within the UK who can boast this level of success.

235
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:49,000
And we're really proud of everything our team leaders do to deliver on these elements of our culture to everybody who work with us.

236
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:50,000
Thanks, Becky.

237
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:56,000
Now, it's no secret that women are hugely underrepresented in the technology industry, which is a great shame.

238
00:21:56,000 --> 00:22:02,000
They're missing out on really rewarding work and we're missing out on all the value they could bring to us.

239
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:07,000
Lots of companies talk about diversity. Sundus is going to tell us what we're actually doing to rectify the situation.

240
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:12,000
Hi, everyone.

241
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:17,000
This year, Portswiger successfully launched its BIRT's Women in Tech Scholarship Scheme.

242
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:32,000
The scholarship is aimed at encouraging young female students to pursue a career in technology and help us bridge the gap of gender disparity in the world of tech right now.

243
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:44,000
This scholarship includes extensive opportunities for paid work, a tailored personal development program, generous financial support throughout the uni, and the potential for a permanent position after graduation.

244
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:50,000
We are really excited and looking forward to providing more opportunities for students in the future. Thank you.

245
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:57,000
Thanks Sundus.

246
00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:06,000
I hope you noticed our office is pretty incredible. Our ambition is to provide the best workplace experience in the Northwest.

247
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:10,000
And as with everything we do, we also aim to be a force for good in the world.

248
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:13,000
And John is going to describe one way we're approaching that.

249
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:27,000
Hi. So in the kitchen, we promote sustainability with local suppliers in frequent deliveries using electric vehicles.

250
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:31,000
We work with a local farm shop, Lamin Shed, to source seasonal produce when it's at its best.

251
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:40,000
Just recently, this is Jack showing one of the next stages in our journey, and he's foraging for things for us.

252
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:45,000
So we've had nettles for risottos, wild garlic for soups, edible flowers for garnishes.

253
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:51,000
The whole concept of this one man and his basket approach is to reduce our carbon footprint and the amount of packaging going into landfill.

254
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:52,000
Thank you.

255
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:59,000
Thanks, John.

256
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:04,000
We love data at Port Swigger, and Anthony loves data more than anyone else.

257
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
And he's got a question for you.

258
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:17,000
So, as is the old data theme, I'm going to have a major quiz for you guys.

259
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:20,000
I'm going to ask just one question. It's going to be a show of hands for the answers.

260
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:26,000
Our question is up on the screen, which is, for each Port Swigger employee, how many BURP users do we have?

261
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:32,000
So I just want you to raise your hands if you think it's A. Raise your hands. No, no one.

262
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:41,000
B. Got someone over there. We've got C. Cool. Got a few on C. And how about D? Cool.

263
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:45,000
So if you've got your hand up for D, keep it up, because unfortunately you're wrong. The answer is actually C.

264
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:50,000
Yeah, so for each of your children, there are 710 users, which is pretty bloody impressive.

265
00:24:50,000 --> 00:25:02,000
Thanks, Anthony. Now, we are normally rather understated when it comes to talking about our success.

266
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:06,000
But while we're on the subject of numbers, there are a few others that are worth sharing.

267
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:12,000
We sell our software to 16,000 organisations in 170 countries.

268
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:16,000
We've grown over 25% every year for more than a decade.

269
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:20,000
There are 100 of us, and we all work out of this one office.

270
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:24,000
We're obviously very happy to be so successful commercially,

271
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:29,000
and Frankie's going to tell us about one way we all share the benefits of that.

272
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:39,000
So one of the many, many benefits that we get here at Portswigger is that we are all granted share options.

273
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:44,000
Now, these aren't given just to the top dogs, and that's simply because here we are all the top dogs.

274
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:49,000
So, Swiggers are granted these after being here a year, and every year thereafter.

275
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:53,000
This gives us all the choice to become a shareholder and get a piece of the Portswigger pie,

276
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:55,000
and also receive dividends.

277
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:59,000
So, what better way to show that we are all genuinely really valued as part of the company?

278
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:07,000
Thanks, Frankie. Now we're almost done.

279
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:12,000
We've heard about all the amazing things that your children are doing.

280
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:17,000
Solving hard problems, delighting customers, enabling the world to skew the web,

281
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:20,000
building a healthy culture, being successful.

282
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:24,000
Now, for me, all of this leads to one thing, which is that we have fun.

283
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:33,000
I started working on Burp purely for fun, and that motivation hasn't changed.

284
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:37,000
I think we can genuinely say that Portswigger is a fun place to be.

285
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:42,000
I hope you've also had fun here today, more than at a school parents' evening.

286
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:45,000
Before we wrap up, does anyone have any questions?

287
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:54,000
The origin of the company name?

288
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,000
So, the question was, what's the origin of the company name?

289
00:26:56,000 --> 00:27:02,000
So, a long time ago, when I started learning how to be a security tester,

290
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:05,000
this was before it was regarded as a legitimate pursuit.

291
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:08,000
And I was, you know, it was my job. I was working for a big reputable company,

292
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:10,000
but they didn't really want to be associated with this activity,

293
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:15,000
because the wider world didn't recognise it as a good thing to do.

294
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:20,000
Also, this was before there were any decent resources, any books, any blogs,

295
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:23,000
anywhere you could go to really learn from others how to do it.

296
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:27,000
So, you have to half figure it out for yourself and half ask questions to the community.

297
00:27:27,000 --> 00:27:31,000
So, this was before things like Twitter, you were on bulletin boards and mailing lists

298
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:32,000
where you would post questions.

299
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:35,000
And generally, they said it's a bad idea to post under your real name

300
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:39,000
because you don't really want to be outed as one of them.

301
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:43,000
Now, you know, so you needed a handle and everyone had a hacking handle.

302
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:46,000
And when I started hacking, it was before the days of the modern web.

303
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:51,000
So, we were testing infrastructure, networks, firewalls, that sort of thing.

304
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:56,000
And a lot of the tools had the word port in the name, things like port scanner.

305
00:27:56,000 --> 00:28:00,000
So, it was a bit of a silly pun. Port is also a drink and swigger means drinker,

306
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:01,000
and it sounded a bit like port scanner.

307
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:05,000
So, in about 30 seconds of consideration, I adopted that as my handle

308
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:07,000
and used it when I asked questions.

309
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:09,000
And then when I started to write the software,

310
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:12,000
I didn't want to out myself as the author either.

311
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:15,000
So, it was initially released anonymously under that handle.

312
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:18,000
And then, eventually, it turned into a real business.

313
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:23,000
Now, there are numerous points in our history where we could,

314
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:27,000
and there's another, you know, another similarly silly reason why Burp has got a silly name.

315
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:30,000
There are various points in our journey where we could have decided,

316
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:34,000
now's the time to grow up and get a more sensible name.

317
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:38,000
But it always felt like we've accumulated quite a lot of brand awareness

318
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:40,000
and goodwill towards this name.

319
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:42,000
So, let's stick with it a bit longer.

320
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:44,000
And that's still the case today.

321
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:47,000
And a few years ago, we did actually ask some brand consultants

322
00:28:47,000 --> 00:28:51,000
if these silly names were the right choice for us.

323
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:53,000
And they went and we paid them a lot of money,

324
00:28:53,000 --> 00:28:54,000
and they did a lot of recon and work.

325
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:57,000
And they came back and said, actually, they're working really well.

326
00:28:57,000 --> 00:28:59,000
Because if you look across our competitors,

327
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:03,000
they have names that are all about the same with like SIS and SEC,

328
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:05,000
and names and things like that in the...

329
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:06,000
Well, you stand out. It's a big list of vendors.

330
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:08,000
It's like Portswigger and it's bright orange.

331
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:10,000
What on earth is that?

332
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:12,000
So, they told us to stick with it, which is good,

333
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:13,000
because we weren't going to change it anyway.

334
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:31,000
Yes, it's where we'd like to be in five years time.

335
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:33,000
It is a good question.

336
00:29:33,000 --> 00:29:35,000
I think, in all honesty to us,

337
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:37,000
it's a bit beyond our normal planning horizon.

338
00:29:37,000 --> 00:29:41,000
We tend to think maybe a year, a little bit longer ahead.

339
00:29:41,000 --> 00:29:44,000
We have ideas sometimes for long-term initiatives,

340
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:47,000
and we have a few at the moment that we would probably like to do.

341
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:51,000
So, another addition to Burp Suite aimed at the developer space,

342
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:54,000
probably a web-based scan-as-a-service platform,

343
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:56,000
so you can come and subscribe and be scanned

344
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:58,000
without having to download and install the software.

345
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:01,000
But some of our other initiatives like the Academy and certifications

346
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:04,000
and the Daily Swig, we kind of thought about them for a while,

347
00:30:04,000 --> 00:30:07,000
but then only decided almost just in time,

348
00:30:07,000 --> 00:30:10,000
let's go ahead and now we're ready.

349
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:14,000
I think we'll stay fairly firmly in web security,

350
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:18,000
but that's in itself such a rich landscape of things we can do

351
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:21,000
and go after and spread out into.

352
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:23,000
Over time, we've done more adjacent things,

353
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:25,000
so I think we probably will do some others,

354
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:27,000
and what exactly they are, we'll decide a bit later.

355
00:30:27,000 --> 00:30:29,000
We'll decide a bit as we go.

356
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:33,000
But one thing I'm pretty confident of is that our growth trajectory

357
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:35,000
in terms of the team and our people

358
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:39,000
will be a fairly continuous evolution of where we are now.

359
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:41,000
We've got an amazing, massive building.

360
00:30:41,000 --> 00:30:43,000
That was to give us headroom to grow into,

361
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:45,000
because we didn't relish the upheaval

362
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:47,000
of having to keep moving office every few years.

363
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:50,000
But we're not expecting an explosion of numbers.

364
00:30:50,000 --> 00:30:53,000
That's partly because we want exceptional people,

365
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:55,000
and there's only so many of them that we run into,

366
00:30:55,000 --> 00:30:57,000
and we're so protective of our culture,

367
00:30:57,000 --> 00:31:00,000
so it takes a while to absorb a new person into a team

368
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:04,000
and make sure that they've landed well and have got our view of the world.

369
00:31:04,000 --> 00:31:09,000
So I'm expecting us to grow our capabilities in a fairly continuous way,

370
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:11,000
do more things, do as well as we possibly can,

371
00:31:11,000 --> 00:31:13,000
and almost more important than anything else,

372
00:31:13,000 --> 00:31:17,000
hold on to the amazing culture that we've got as we grow.

373
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:21,000
So I think if you look now into any team of around 10 people,

374
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:25,000
it looks and feels in itself more or less what Pulse would look like

375
00:31:25,000 --> 00:31:27,000
when the whole business was 10 people.

376
00:31:27,000 --> 00:31:30,000
So if we grow tenfold again, we might have 100 teams,

377
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:35,000
but each one hopefully will look about the same as that if we've been successful.

378
00:31:35,000 --> 00:31:45,000
Don't be shy.

379
00:31:45,000 --> 00:31:51,000
Okay, well, that's the end of this session.

380
00:31:51,000 --> 00:31:54,000
The plan now is to get a drink and a snack.

381
00:31:54,000 --> 00:31:56,000
You can have a look around, explore the building,

382
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:58,000
you can go and see where your child works, meet their friends,

383
00:31:58,000 --> 00:32:00,000
ask any other questions you might have.

384
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:02,000
Thank you very much.

385
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:06,000
Thank you.

